---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import PageShell from "../components/PageShell.astro";
import { site } from "../config";

const title = "Privacy — LLM Left";
const description = "See exactly what LLM Left keeps on your Mac, what it can sync through your private CloudKit database, and what the website does not collect.";
const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "WebPage",
  name: title,
  url: `${site.url}/privacy/`,
  description,
  isPartOf: { "@type": "WebSite", name: site.name, url: site.url },
};
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} path="/privacy/" schema={schema}>
  <PageShell eyebrow="Privacy" title="The exact data boundary." intro="LLM Left is designed to move a small usage snapshot between your Apple devices without moving the secrets or content behind it.">
    <div class="privacy-table" role="table" aria-label="LLM Left data boundary">
      <div class="privacy-table-head" role="row">
        <span role="columnheader">Stays on your Mac</span>
        <span role="columnheader">Can sync through private CloudKit</span>
      </div>
      <div class="privacy-table-body" role="row">
        <ul role="cell">
          <li>Provider credentials and login files</li>
          <li>Prompts and conversations</li>
          <li>Other command-line output</li>
          <li>The local bridge token</li>
          <li>Detailed usage history</li>
          <li>Account identity hashes and operation records</li>
          <li>The OpenRouter management key</li>
        </ul>
        <ul role="cell">
          <li>Provider and plan labels</li>
          <li>Remaining and used percentages</li>
          <li>Limit names and windows</li>
          <li>Reset dates and forecasts</li>
          <li>Snapshot time and pace</li>
          <li>Account names, states, and check times</li>
          <li>OpenRouter credit and spend totals</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="prose-block">
      <h2>Your private iCloud account</h2>
      <p>The synced snapshot uses your private CloudKit database. LLM Left does not run an account service, and it does not send the snapshot to an LLM Left server.</p>

      <h2>Which Mac publishes</h2>
      <p>Only a Mac with publishing turned on in Settings > iCloud Sync writes the snapshot. A second Mac can turn publishing off and keep reading usage for its own menu bar without touching the shared record.</p>

      <h2>Multiple accounts</h2>
      <p>Each managed account keeps its native login files and identity hash on the Mac. Its name, quota windows, check times, and status can appear in the snapshot, so use account names you are comfortable syncing. Removing an account from monitoring does not remove its login files. Optional window-start requests send a small fixed prompt to the provider and use quota; they are off by default and never spend a reset credit.</p>

      <h2>No product analytics</h2>
      <p>The app does not send usage analytics. This website has no analytics script, advertising pixel, session recorder, or third-party font request.</p>

      <h2>Software updates</h2>
      <p>The Mac app uses Sparkle to check the LLM Left update service. As with a normal web request, the update host can receive an IP address and user agent. It does not receive your provider usage, prompts, or CloudKit data.</p>

      <h2>Local permissions</h2>
      <p>The Mac app runs a signed local helper on <code>127.0.0.1</code> to read supported provider usage. It does not require a VPN, a Tailscale network, device pairing, or an LLM Left account.</p>

      <h2>Questions</h2>
      <p>For a privacy issue, use the support path included with the installed app. This page will be updated if the product data boundary changes.</p>
    </div>
  </PageShell>
</Layout>
